 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L10_001_Your First Class",
   "display_name": "Your First Class",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 10! So far we have used types that Python gives us: <code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>dict</code>... A <b>class</b> lets you define <b>your own type</b>. Think of a class as a <b>blueprint</b>, and the <b>objects</b> made from it as the actual things built from that blueprint:</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Dog</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">name</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">age</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">bark()</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;padding:22px 6px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">&rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">d1 : Dog</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">name = &#x27;Rex&#x27;</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">age = 3</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">d2 : Dog</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">name = &#x27;Tommy&#x27;</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">age = 5</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">one class (blueprint), two objects (instances), each with its own values</div>\n<p>A class is defined with the keyword <code>class</code>. Inside it, a special function called <b><code>__init__</code></b> (two underscores on each side) runs automatically <b>every time a new object is created</b>. It is called the <b>constructor</b>, and its job is to give the new object its starting values, called <b>attributes</b>:</p>\n<pre>class Dog:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name      # an attribute of this object\n        self.age = age\n\nd1 = Dog(&quot;Rex&quot;, 3)            # creates an object: __init__ runs\nd2 = Dog(&quot;Tommy&quot;, 5)\nprint(d1.name, d1.age)\nprint(d2.name, d2.age)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Rex 3\nTommy 5</pre>\n<p><b><code>self</code></b> is the object being built (or used). <code>self.name = name</code> stores the value <b>inside that object</b>. You never pass <code>self</code> yourself: <code>Dog(\"Rex\", 3)</code> gives <code>\"Rex\"</code> to <code>name</code> and <code>3</code> to <code>age</code>. Read an attribute with a dot: <code>d1.name</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Book</code> whose constructor takes <code>title</code> and <code>author</code>, and stores them as the attributes <code>title</code> and <code>author</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>b = Book(\"Malgudi Days\", \"R. K. Narayan\")</code>, <code>b.title</code> is <code>\"Malgudi Days\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Book:\n    def __init__(self, title, author):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# b = Book(\"Malgudi Days\", \"R. K. Narayan\")\n# print(b.title, \"by\", b.author)\n",
