  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L10_004_Printing An Object",
   "display_name": "Printing An Object",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Printing an object normally shows something unhelpful like <code>&lt;__main__.Point object at 0x7f...&gt;</code>. To control what <code>print</code> shows, define the special method <b><code>__str__</code></b>, which must <b>return a string</b>:</p>\n<pre>class Temperature:\n    def __init__(self, degrees):\n        self.degrees = degrees\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return str(self.degrees) + &quot; C&quot;\n\nt = Temperature(31)\nprint(t)\nprint(&quot;Today:&quot;, t)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>31 C\nToday: 31 C</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Point</code> whose constructor takes <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, and whose <code>__str__</code> returns the text <code>(x, y)</code> with the actual values.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>print(Point(3, -1))</code> prints <code>(3, -1)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(Point(3, -1))\n",
