  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L10_005_Changing And Deleting Attributes",
   "display_name": "Changing And Deleting Attributes",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Attributes can be read, changed, <b>added</b> and <b>deleted</b> from outside the class:</p>\n<pre>class Pet:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\np = Pet(&quot;Bruno&quot;)\np.name = &quot;Max&quot;          # change\np.age = 2               # add a new attribute to this object only\nprint(p.name, p.age)\ndel p.age               # delete it again\nprint(hasattr(p, &#x27;age&#x27;))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Max 2\nFalse</pre>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>hasattr(obj, \"attr\")</code> is <code>True</code> if the object has that attribute.</p>\n<p>(Changing attributes from outside is allowed, but it is usually better to do it through <b>methods</b>, as you will see next.)</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Pet</code> is given. Write the function <code>rename(pet, new_name)</code>, which changes the pet's <code>name</code> to <code>new_name</code> and also gives the pet an attribute <code>old_name</code> holding its previous name. It returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>p = Pet(\"Bruno\")</code> and <code>rename(p, \"Max\")</code>, <code>p.name</code> is <code>\"Max\"</code> and <code>p.old_name</code> is <code>\"Bruno\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Pet:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n\ndef rename(pet, new_name):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# p = Pet(\"Bruno\")\n# rename(p, \"Max\")\n# print(p.name, p.old_name)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
