  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L10_006_Your First Method",
   "display_name": "Your First Method",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>method</b> is a function defined <b>inside</b> a class. Its first parameter is always <code>self</code>, the object it is called on, so it can use the object's attributes. You call a method with a dot: <code>obj.method(...)</code>; again, <code>self</code> is passed automatically.</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Square</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">side</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">area()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">perimeter()</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">the class now has attributes AND methods</div>\n<pre>class Square:\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side * self.side\n\n    def perimeter(self):\n        return 4 * self.side\n\ns = Square(5)\nprint(s.area(), s.perimeter())</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>25 20</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Circle</code> whose constructor takes <code>r</code> (the radius), with two methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>area()</code> returns <code>3.14 * r * r</code>, rounded to 2 decimal places with <code>round(value, 2)</code>;</li><li><code>diameter()</code> returns <code>2 * r</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>Circle(10).area()</code> returns <code>314.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Circle:\n    def __init__(self, r):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def area(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def diameter(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# c = Circle(10)\n# print(c.area(), c.diameter())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
