  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "L10_007_Methods That Change The Object",
   "display_name": "Methods That Change The Object",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Methods can <b>change</b> the object's attributes. This is the usual way to change an object: the class decides what changes are allowed.</p>\n<pre>class Lamp:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.on = False\n\n    def switch(self):\n        self.on = not self.on\n\nl = Lamp()\nl.switch()\nprint(l.on)\nl.switch()\nprint(l.on)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>True\nFalse</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>BankAccount</code> whose constructor takes <code>owner</code> and an optional <code>balance</code> (default <code>0</code>), with the methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>deposit(amount)</code> adds <code>amount</code> to the balance;</li><li><code>withdraw(amount)</code> takes <code>amount</code> out of the balance <b>only if</b> the balance is enough, and returns <code>True</code>; otherwise it changes nothing and returns <code>False</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, after <code>a = BankAccount(\"Asha\", 100)</code>, <code>a.withdraw(150)</code> returns <code>False</code>, and <code>a.balance</code> is still <code>100</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# a = BankAccount(\"Asha\", 100)\n# a.deposit(50)\n# print(a.withdraw(30), a.balance)\n",
