  {
   "xml_index": 8,
   "xml_name": "L10_008_Methods Calling Methods",
   "display_name": "Methods Calling Methods",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Inside a class, one method can call another method of the same object with <code>self.method(...)</code>. This avoids repeating code:</p>\n<pre>class Rectangle:\n    def __init__(self, w, h):\n        self.w = w\n        self.h = h\n\n    def area(self):\n        return self.w * self.h\n\n    def bigger_than(self, other):\n        return self.area() &gt; other.area()     # uses area() of both\n\nprint(Rectangle(4, 5).bigger_than(Rectangle(3, 6)))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>True</pre>\n<p>Note that a method can take <b>another object</b> as a parameter (<code>other</code>) and use its methods too.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Temperature</code> whose constructor takes <code>celsius</code>, with the methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>fahrenheit()</code> returns <code>celsius * 9 / 5 + 32</code>;</li><li><code>feels()</code> returns <code>\"hot\"</code> if the Fahrenheit value is more than 95, <code>\"cold\"</code> if it is less than 50, and <code>\"pleasant\"</code> otherwise. It must call <code>fahrenheit()</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>Temperature(40).feels()</code> returns <code>\"hot\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Temperature:\n    def __init__(self, celsius):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def fahrenheit(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def feels(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# t = Temperature(40)\n# print(t.fahrenheit(), t.feels())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
