  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L10_009_Keeping The Object Valid",
   "display_name": "Keeping The Object Valid",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Methods can <b>check</b> the values they are given, so that an object never ends up in a wrong state (for example, marks outside 0 to 100). This is a big advantage of changing objects through methods rather than directly.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Student</code> whose constructor takes <code>name</code>; each student starts with an empty list of marks (attribute <code>marks</code>). Methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>add_mark(m)</code> adds <code>m</code> to the list and returns <code>True</code> if <code>m</code> is from 0 to 100; otherwise it ignores <code>m</code> and returns <code>False</code>;</li><li><code>average()</code> returns the average of the marks rounded to 2 decimal places with <code>round(value, 2)</code>, or <code>0</code> if there are no marks.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, after <code>s = Student(\"Asha\")</code>, <code>s.add_mark(120)</code> returns <code>False</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def add_mark(self, m):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def average(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# s = Student(\"Asha\")\n# print(s.add_mark(90), s.add_mark(120), s.add_mark(75))\n# print(s.marks, s.average())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "s = Student(\"Asha\")\nprint(s.add_mark(90), s.add_mark(120), s.add_mark(75))\nprint(s.marks, s.average())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True False True\n[90, 75] 82.5",
