  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L10_011_Returning New Objects",
   "display_name": "Returning New Objects",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A method can create and <b>return a new object</b> of the same class, leaving the original unchanged (like <code>sorted</code> versus <code>sort</code>). A method can also take another object of the same class as a parameter.</p>\n<pre>class Vector:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    def plus(self, other):\n        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n\nv = Vector(1, 2).plus(Vector(10, 20))\nprint(v.x, v.y)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>11 22</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Point</code> whose constructor takes <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, with:</p>\n<ul><li><code>moved(dx, dy)</code> returning a <b>new</b> <code>Point</code> at <code>(x + dx, y + dy)</code> (the original does not change);</li><li><code>distance_to(other)</code> returning the distance to the point <code>other</code>, <code>((x2 - x1)<b>2 + (y2 - y1)</b>2) ** 0.5</code>, rounded to 2 decimal places.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>Point(0, 0).distance_to(Point(3, 4))</code> returns <code>5.0</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def moved(self, dx, dy):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def distance_to(self, other):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# p = Point(1, 2)\n# q = p.moved(2, 2)\n# print(q.x, q.y, p.x, p.y)\n# print(p.distance_to(q))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "p = Point(1, 2)\nq = p.moved(2, 2)\nprint(q.x, q.y, p.x, p.y)\nprint(p.distance_to(q))",
