  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L10_012_Class Attributes",
   "display_name": "Class Attributes",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Attributes set with <code>self.x = ...</code> belong to <b>one object</b> (instance attributes). A variable written directly inside the class body belongs to the <b>class itself</b> and is <b>shared by all objects</b>: a <b>class attribute</b>. Read it as <code>ClassName.attr</code> (or through any object).</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Car</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">wheels = 4   (shared)</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\">&nbsp;</div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;padding:22px 6px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">&rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">c1 : Car</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">colour = &#x27;red&#x27;</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:1px solid #2a8c8c;border-radius:10px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:120px;background:#eef7f7\"><div style=\"padding:3px 10px;font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#1d6464;border-bottom:1px solid #b9dcdc\">c2 : Car</div><div style=\"padding:4px 10px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">colour = &#x27;blue&#x27;</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">both cars see Car.wheels; each has its own colour</div>\n<pre>class Car:\n    wheels = 4                 # class attribute\n\n    def __init__(self, colour):\n        self.colour = colour   # instance attribute\n\na = Car(&quot;red&quot;)\nb = Car(&quot;blue&quot;)\nprint(a.wheels, b.wheels, Car.wheels)\nCar.wheels = 6              # changes it for everyone\nprint(a.wheels, b.wheels)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>4 4 4\n6 6</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Circle</code> with a class attribute <code>PI = 3.14</code>, a constructor taking <code>r</code>, and a method <code>area()</code> that returns <code>Circle.PI * r * r</code> rounded to 2 decimal places. Because <code>PI</code> is a class attribute, changing <code>Circle.PI</code> must change the result for all circles.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>Circle(1).area()</code> returns <code>3.14</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define the class Circle with the class attribute PI = 3.14.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(Circle(1).area())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(Circle(1).area())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "3.14",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(Circle(2).area(), Circle.PI)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "12.56 3.14",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "a = Circle(10)\nb = Circle(1)\nCircle.PI = 3.14159\nprint(a.area(), b.area())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "314.16 3.14",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(Circle(0).area())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0.0",
     "extra": "",
