  {
   "xml_index": 14,
   "xml_name": "L10_014_Instance Or Class Attribute",
   "display_name": "Instance Or Class Attribute",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A trap: <b>assigning</b> through an object, <code>obj.attr = value</code>, always creates or changes an <b>instance</b> attribute, even if a class attribute has the same name. The class attribute is then hidden for that object only:</p>\n<pre>class Game:\n    lives = 3\n\ng1 = Game()\ng2 = Game()\ng1.lives = 1          # a NEW instance attribute on g1 only\nprint(g1.lives, g2.lives, Game.lives)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>1 3 3</pre>\n<p>So: use <code>ClassName.attr</code> to read or change <b>shared</b> values, and <code>self.attr</code> for each object's <b>own</b> values.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Player</code> with a class attribute <code>start_lives = 3</code>. The constructor takes <code>name</code> and gives the player its own attribute <code>lives</code>, equal to the class's <code>start_lives</code> <b>at the moment the player is created</b>. The method <code>lose_life()</code> takes one life away (never going below 0) and returns the lives left.</p>\n<p>For example, if <code>Player.start_lives</code> is changed to 5, players created afterwards start with 5 lives, but existing players keep theirs.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define the class Player.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# p = Player(\"Asha\")\n# print(p.lose_life(), p.lives)\n",
