  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L10_015_Constructors And Destructors",
   "display_name": "Constructors And Destructors",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>constructor</b> <code>__init__</code> runs when an object is <b>created</b>. Python also has a <b>destructor</b>, <code>__del__</code>, which runs when an object is about to be <b>destroyed</b>: when nothing refers to it any more. It is used to \"clean up\" (close a file, print a goodbye, ...).</p>\n<p><code>del x</code> removes the <b>name</b> <code>x</code>. If that was the last name referring to the object, the object is destroyed and <code>__del__</code> runs. If another name still refers to it, the object lives on:</p>\n<pre>class Robot:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        print(&quot;Hello from&quot;, name)\n\n    def __del__(self):\n        print(&quot;Goodbye from&quot;, self.name)\n\nr = Robot(&quot;R1&quot;)\ndel r                  # last name gone: destroyed now\na = Robot(&quot;R2&quot;)\nb = a                  # two names, one object\ndel a                  # b still refers to it: no goodbye yet\nprint(&quot;still here:&quot;, b.name)\ndel b\nprint(&quot;end&quot;)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Hello from R1\nGoodbye from R1\nHello from R2\nstill here: R2\nGoodbye from R2\nend</pre>\n<p><b>Careful:</b> you do not control exactly when Python destroys objects in general (for example, objects still alive when the program ends). So do not rely on <code>__del__</code> for important work. In the tests here, objects are always deleted explicitly with <code>del</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Session</code> whose constructor takes <code>user</code>, stores it and prints <code>login &lt;user&gt;</code>, and whose destructor prints <code>logout &lt;user&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>s = Session(\"asha\")</code> followed by <code>del s</code> prints <code>login asha</code> and then <code>logout asha</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define the class Session with __init__ and __del__.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# s = Session(\"asha\")\n# del s\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "s = Session(\"asha\")\ndel s\nprint(\"done\")",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "login asha\nlogout asha\ndone",
