  {
   "xml_index": 16,
   "xml_name": "L10_016_A List Of Objects",
   "display_name": "A List Of Objects",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Objects can be stored in lists like any other value, and a loop can use each object's attributes and methods:</p>\n<pre>class City:\n    def __init__(self, name, population):\n        self.name = name\n        self.population = population\n\ncities = [City(&quot;Panaji&quot;, 114), City(&quot;Pune&quot;, 3124), City(&quot;Agra&quot;, 1585)]\nfor c in cities:\n    print(c.name, c.population)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Panaji 114\nPune 3124\nAgra 1585</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Student</code> (with attributes <code>name</code> and <code>marks</code>) is given. Write the function <code>topper(students)</code>, which returns the <b>name</b> of the student with the highest marks in the non-empty list <code>students</code> (the first one on a tie).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>topper([Student(\"Asha\", 82), Student(\"Ravi\", 91)])</code> returns <code>\"Ravi\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name, marks):\n        self.name = name\n        self.marks = marks\n\n\ndef topper(students):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(topper([Student(\"Asha\", 82), Student(\"Ravi\", 91)]))\n",
