  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "L10_019_Shopping Cart",
   "display_name": "Shopping Cart",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Objects often keep their data in lists or dictionaries. A shopping cart can store a dictionary from item names to quantities, and use a price list to compute the total.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a class <code>Cart</code> whose constructor takes <code>prices</code> (a dictionary from item names to prices) and starts with an empty dictionary <code>items</code>. Methods:</p>\n<ul><li><code>add(name, qty=1)</code> adds <code>qty</code> of the item (adding to any quantity already in the cart);</li><li><code>remove(name)</code> removes the item completely if it is in the cart;</li><li><code>total()</code> returns the total cost of the cart.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, with prices <code>{\"tea\": 10, \"samosa\": 15}</code>, adding 2 teas and 1 samosa gives a total of <code>35</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define the class Cart.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# c = Cart({\"tea\": 10, \"samosa\": 15})\n# c.add(\"tea\", 2)\n# c.add(\"samosa\")\n# print(c.items, c.total())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "c = Cart({\"tea\": 10, \"samosa\": 15})\nc.add(\"tea\", 2)\nc.add(\"samosa\")\nprint(c.items, c.total())",
