  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L10_020_A Stack Class",
   "display_name": "A Stack Class",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A <b>stack</b> is a pile: you can only add (<b>push</b>) to the top and take (<b>pop</b>) from the top, like a pile of plates. The last thing pushed is the first popped. A class can wrap a list to offer exactly these operations:</p>\n<pre>class Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.items.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.items.pop()\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return len(self.items) == 0\n\ns = Stack()\ns.push(1)\ns.push(2)\nprint(s.pop(), s.pop(), s.is_empty())</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>2 1 True</pre>\n<p>A classic use: checking that brackets are <b>balanced</b>. Read the text left to right; push every opening bracket; for every closing bracket, the stack must not be empty and its top must be the matching opening bracket (pop it). At the end the stack must be empty.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Stack</code> above is given. Write the function <code>balanced(text)</code>, which returns <code>True</code> if the brackets <code>()</code>, <code>[]</code> and <code>{}</code> in <code>text</code> are balanced (other characters are ignored), and <code>False</code> otherwise. Use a <code>Stack</code> object.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>balanced(\"a[b(c)]\")</code> returns <code>True</code> and <code>balanced(\"(]\")</code> returns <code>False</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.items.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.items.pop()\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return len(self.items) == 0\n\n\ndef balanced(text):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(balanced(\"a[b(c)]\"), balanced(\"(]\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(balanced(\"a[b(c)]\"), balanced(\"(]\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True False",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(balanced(\"\"), balanced(\"((\"), balanced(\"))\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True False False",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(balanced(\"{[()()]}\"), balanced(\"{[(])}\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "True False",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(balanced(\"x = (1 + [2 * 3]) }\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "False",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 21,
   "xml_name": "L10_021_A Subclass",
   "display_name": "A Subclass",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>Inheritance</b> lets you make a new class from an existing one. The new class (the <b>subclass</b>, or child) gets all the attributes and methods of the old one (the <b>superclass</b>, or parent), and can add new ones or <b>replace</b> (override) some:</p>\n<div style=\"margin:6px 0 4px\"><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Animal</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">name</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">speak()</div><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">describe()</div></div></div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;padding:22px 6px 0 0;color:#8a939c\">&rarr;</div><div style=\"display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;border:2px solid #2b4c7e;border-radius:4px;margin:4px 10px 4px 0;min-width:140px\"><div style=\"background:#2b4c7e;color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;font-weight:600\">class Dog(Animal)</div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #c9d3de\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">(inherits name)</div></div><div style=\"padding:3px 8px\"><div style=\"font-family:monospace;font-size:13px\">speak()  (overridden)</div></div></div></div><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">a Dog IS an Animal: it has everything an Animal has</div>\n<pre>class Animal:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def speak(self):\n        return &quot;...&quot;\n\n    def describe(self):\n        return self.name + &quot; says &quot; + self.speak()\n\nclass Dog(Animal):          # Dog inherits from Animal\n    def speak(self):         # override\n        return &quot;Woof&quot;\n\nprint(Animal(&quot;Generic&quot;).describe())\nprint(Dog(&quot;Rex&quot;).describe())</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Generic says ...\nRex says Woof</pre>\n<p>Notice that <code>describe()</code> is written only once, in <code>Animal</code>, but calls the <b>Dog's</b> own <code>speak()</code> for a dog.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Animal</code> above is given. Write two subclasses of <code>Animal</code>: <code>Cat</code>, whose <code>speak()</code> returns <code>\"Meow\"</code>, and <code>Cow</code>, whose <code>speak()</code> returns <code>\"Moo\"</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>Cat(\"Tom\").describe()</code> returns <code>\"Tom says Meow\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Animal:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"...\"\n\n    def describe(self):\n        return self.name + \" says \" + self.speak()\n\n\n# write the classes Cat and Cow here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(Cat(\"Tom\").describe())\n# print(Cow(\"Gauri\").describe())\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(Cat(\"Tom\").describe())\nprint(Cow(\"Gauri\").describe())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Tom says Meow\nGauri says Moo",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "c = Cat(\"Kitty\")\nprint(c.name, c.speak(), isinstance(c, Animal))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Kitty Meow True",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(Animal(\"X\").describe(), Cow(\"Y\").speak())",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "X says ... Moo",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 22,
   "xml_name": "L10_022_Calling The Parent Constructor",
   "display_name": "Calling The Parent Constructor",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>If a subclass needs <b>extra attributes</b>, it defines its own <code>__init__</code>. To still set up everything the parent sets up, it calls the parent's constructor with <b><code>super().__init__(...)</code></b>:</p>\n<pre>class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\nclass Employee(Person):\n    def __init__(self, name, salary):\n        super().__init__(name)      # the Person part\n        self.salary = salary        # the extra part\n\ne = Employee(&quot;Asha&quot;, 50000)\nprint(e.name, e.salary)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Asha 50000</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Person</code> (with attribute <code>name</code> and a <code>__str__</code> returning the name) is given. Write a subclass <code>Student</code> whose constructor takes <code>name</code> and <code>roll</code>, calls the parent constructor for the name, and stores <code>roll</code>. Override <code>__str__</code> to return <code>name (roll)</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>print(Student(\"Asha\", \"23B001\"))</code> prints <code>Asha (23B001)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Person:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.name\n\n\nclass Student(Person):\n    def __init__(self, name, roll):\n        # write your code here\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(Student(\"Asha\", \"23B001\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(Student(\"Asha\", \"23B001\"))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Asha (23B001)",
