  {
   "xml_index": 23,
   "xml_name": "L10_023_Many Shapes One Loop",
   "display_name": "Many Shapes One Loop",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>When several subclasses override the same method, a loop can call that method on a mixed list of objects, and <b>each object uses its own version</b>. This is called <b>polymorphism</b> (\"many forms\"):</p>\n<pre>for s in shapes:\n    total = total + s.area()     # a rectangle&#x27;s area() or a circle&#x27;s area()</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> the class <code>Shape</code> (whose <code>area()</code> returns 0) is given. Write two subclasses:</p>\n<ul><li><code>Rect(w, h)</code> whose <code>area()</code> returns <code>w * h</code>;</li><li><code>Circle(r)</code> whose <code>area()</code> returns <code>3.14 * r * r</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>Then write the function <code>total_area(shapes)</code>, which returns the sum of the areas of the shapes in the list, rounded to 2 decimal places.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>total_area([Rect(2, 3), Circle(1)])</code> returns <code>9.14</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "class Shape:\n    def area(self):\n        return 0\n\n\n# write the classes Rect and Circle, and the function total_area, here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(total_area([Rect(2, 3), Circle(1)]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(total_area([Rect(2, 3), Circle(1)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "9.14",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(total_area([]), total_area([Shape()]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0 0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(total_area([Circle(2), Circle(2), Rect(1, 1)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "26.12",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(total_area([Rect(10, 0), Rect(0.5, 4)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "2.0",
     "extra": "",
